  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...\nThe overall weather pattern today through the weekend will be \ndriven by a sharp upper level trough digging across the Great \nLakes region. The first of two cold fronts is currently moving\nthrough the area this morning with not much fanfare in our\nweather. There is a broken line of rain showers along this front\nthat will continue to track eastward through sunrise this\nmorning. Our recent very mild temperatures will be knocked down\ncloser to average today into Friday. The second and stronger \ncold front will sweep southward across the region Friday, \nbringing a quick shot of colder weather for the weekend. With\nthe colder air moving over the very mild Great Lakes will also\nbring lake effect cloudiness and scattered lake effect rain\nshowers Friday through Saturday night. It will be a reality\ncheck from our recent stretch of very mild and quiet weather\nthat we have enjoyed since early October. \n\nWe have likely already reached our max temperatures for this 24\nhour day before this cold frontal passage. Temperatures will\ngenerally hold steady or a slow fall later today staying in the\nupper 50s to around 60 degrees. Winds will remain breezy from \nthe south to southwest today 10 to 20 mph. Cooler temperatures \nmove back in overnight falling into the 40s with cloudy skies. \nMainly dry weather for most of the overnight but POPs increase \ntowards the predawn hours as the secondary and stronger cold \nfront starts to move into the region. That cold front will sweep\nacross the area quickly Friday morning followed by a colder \nshot of air Friday through this weekend. The sharp upper level \ntrough will dig over the Great Lakes Friday into Saturday which\nwill bring a more typical fall weather setup that we associate \nwith lake effect clouds as well as scattered rain showers \ndownwind of the lakes. One note worthy weather stat to mention \nis the current Lake Erie water temperature is in the low 60s. \nThat current water temp is running about 8 to 10 degrees above \nnormal for early November. High temps will generally be in the \n50s Friday afternoon and this weekend. Some locations in the \nSnowbelt region may struggle to get out of the 40s on Saturday. \nIt will also be blustery Friday through Saturday night which \nwill also make it feel colder adding a bit of a wind chill \nfactor. Overall, rainfall amounts from the cold front Friday and\nscattered lake effect rain showers through Saturday night will \nbe on the light side, around a tenth to maybe a couple tenths in\nthe Snowbelt of NEOH and NWPA. \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2... \nThere is some better weather retuning in the forecast Sunday\nthrough early or middle of next week. High pressure will build\nback across the Ohio Valley starting Sunday and continue to\ndominate our weather pattern through maybe Wednesday. More\nseasonable and fair weather conditions will return for our area\nto end the weekend and early next week. Temperatures will slowly\nclimb back above average early next week, starting out with 50s\nfor afternoon highs Sunday but 60s come back in the forecast by\nearly next week. Some bigger weather changes may be on the far\nhorizon or just beyond our 7 day forecast period. Model \nguidance is starting to indicate that stronger weather system\nmay develop over the Great Lakes region by the end of next week\nwith a bigger push of colder weather and lake effect precip. \nStay tuned!",
